      // [src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_rolland, src_debussy_mason1918 | 1883-1913 | biography + critic's lecture + independent critic + adversarial critic] Vallas, quoting Maurice Emmanuel's notebook of Debussy's talks with Guiraud, shows Debussy assembling his own harmonic and modal syntax (varied scales, enharmonic ambiguity, ambiguous chords, irregular rhythms) in the 1880s before using it in the Rome submissions, the Faune and the Nocturnes. In Pelleas he deliberately chose continuous declamation modelled on French speech and an orchestra reduced to discreet colours, telling Robert de Flers in 1902 that he wanted no 'parasite' musical phrases and distrusted leitmotifs; Vuillermoz adds that each work received its own plan rather than a stock form, and Vallas says that with La Mer he deliberately did not repeat Pelleas. Non-partisan and adversarial corroboration: the Academie (1886-87) and the critic Maurice Kufferath (1894, quoted in Vallas) found the music strange; Rolland, who says he is 'not a Debussyite', credits a real reform of French recitative and notes the originality lies in a new use of existing chords; Mason, hostile to the aims, concedes originality of harmonic style and orchestration. Kept below the top band because Rolland and Vallas also record borrowings and echoes (Moussorgsky, Chabrier, Charpentier's Louise) and Rolland judges the historical importance larger than the artistic value. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 85/0.72; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (Vallas), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
