      // [src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_laloy1909 | 1883-1915 | biography + critic's lecture + friend's study] Vallas: at the Conservatoire in 1883 Debussy tried chains of fifths and octaves, sevenths that did not resolve, chords of the ninth, eleventh and thirteenth on every degree (Emmanuel); in 1889 he went repeatedly to the Exposition universelle to listen closely to Javanese, Spanish and Chinese ensembles with Paul Dukas and later wrote of Javanese and Annamite music again; with the Nocturnes and Faune he divided the strings and used muted brass and solo timbres in new ways (Vallas, Laloy); in Jeux (1913) he tried superposed seconds close to what Stravinsky had just done in the Rite of Spring, and in 1915 he wrote twelve piano studies, each built on one technical problem. Vuillermoz and Vallas both say he made a fresh plan for each work. Repeated, dated and independent of one another; not scored higher because Vallas notes several of the apparent novelties had precedents. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 80/0.7; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (Vallas), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
