      // [src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_mason1918, src_debussy_liebich1908 | 1880-1913 | biography + critic's lecture + adversarial critic + contemporary biography] Vuillermoz reports he held back the proofs of a song for two years to find the right balance between two bars; Liebich, citing a letter to Laloy, says he would not publish the Suite bergamasque while twelve bars of the Sarabande still dissatisfied him; Vallas, quoting Xavier Leroux and Henri de Regnier, records an attraction in his student years to 'everything refined, delicate, complicated, strange' in clothes, objects, books and food, and his dislike of grandiloquence; he chose texts and titles with literary and pictorial care and insisted the Preludes' titles stand at the end of each piece rather than the head (Vallas). Mason, who disputes the worth of the aim, still calls the 'sheer tonal magic' of his sonorities admitted even by detractors. Behavior (selection, refusal, refinement) rather than reputation. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 84/0.7; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (Vallas), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
