      // [src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_laloy1909, src_debussy_liebich1908 | 1890-1914 | critic's lecture + biography quoting letters + friend's study + contemporary biography] Vuillermoz: he worked slowly, was never in a hurry to release a piece, had to be pressed page by page for the engraver, and delayed one song's proofs two years over two bars. Vallas, quoting his letter of summer 1893 to Chausson: having finished the fourth-act fountain scene of Pelleas he decided it was 'not that at all', that the ghost of Wagner showed at the turn of a bar, and tore it all up to find 'more personal' phrases; he then finished Pelleas twice (1895, 1897), reworked it until 1902 and wrote the orchestration only at the last minute; he withdrew the 1890 Fantasy for piano and orchestra for life after the dress rehearsal and reused material in a piano piece; he abandoned the unfinished opera Chimene and destroyed his notes for the unfinished Poe opera The Devil in the Belfry (Vallas), explaining in a letter that the choral writing he had found did not yet satisfy him. Liebich/Laloy add the Sarabande's missing bars. Impact dual-edged: few works and long delays, and Vallas also records fast commissioned scores (the 1884 cantata in 25 days; Le Martyre de Saint Sebastien in two months), so the standard was applied to his own chosen projects rather than to everything. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 78/0.72; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (Vallas), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
