      // [src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_croche1921 | 1887-1914 | biography + critic's lecture + self essays] In print he attacked institutions and reputations without softening (the Prix de Rome and the Institut, Saint-Saens's opera Les Barbares, the Wagner method; Vallas, Croche), and he accepted the Academie's public rebuke and the booing and mockery at the 1902 Pelleas dress rehearsal without altering the score beyond one cut of 15 bars (Vallas). But the same sources describe a man who avoided face-to-face confrontation: Vuillermoz speaks of a 'haughty misanthropy' behind a rampart of irony; Debussy told Messager he was 'incapable of outward demonstrations' to rally others and after the opening weeks suffered a crisis of discouragement (Vallas); Maeterlinck, angry over the casting, broke with him and publicly wished the opera to fail (Vallas), and the sources record no matching public quarrel on Debussy's side. Mixed picture of written boldness and personal avoidance, so a mid score at modest confidence and dual-edged impact.
