      // [src_debussy_vallas, src_debussy_vuillermoz1920, src_debussy_liebich1908 | 1875-1913 | biography quoting classmates and friends + critic's lecture + contemporary biography] Classmates (Bellaigue, Pierne) remember him at the Conservatoire as shut-in, awkward and brusque; Henri de Regnier says he spoke little of himself but judged colleagues severely and 'kept something distant'; Vuillermoz says playing his own pieces or accompanying singers in public was an ordeal he accepted rarely and with 'sullen timidity', that he never recruited allies or promoted his work, and Vallas notes his visibly nervous, rigid conducting of La Mer in 1908 and his admission to Messager that he could not stir up others' spirits. Liebich calls him unapproachable to strangers though genial among intimates. The same quiet figure later gave blunt interviews and wrote sharp criticism (see conflict tolerance), so the low score is about face-to-face and public self-assertion, not strength of opinion. Several witnesses; most are friends, and 'taciturn' is a repeated characterisation rather than a dated incident, hence documented but modest confidence.
