      // [src_degas_lemoisne1912, src_degas_boggs1988, src_degas_vollard1927, src_degas_moore1906 | 1863-1886 | scholar-acquaintance preface + museum catalogue quoting hostile and friendly contemporaries + dealer and writer memoirs] He held his own line against the group he exhibited with. Lemoisne: he stayed out of the 1863 Salon des Refuses and exhibited at the official Salon until the end of the Empire, then joined the independents in 1874, but rejected the plein-air principle of the Impressionists, painted landscapes and racing scenes in the studio from sketches and memory, and bridled at the 'Impressionist' label. Vollard confirms the studio method (a little wooden horse for a model, 'never say plein air to me again') and Harold Van Doren's introduction to the Vollard volume says he was identified 'against his will' with a school. Boggs: Paul Mantz (Le Temps, 1877) wrote that it was unclear why Degas counted himself among the Impressionists, 'He has a distinct personality and stands apart'; Bertall (1877) said he had 'reserved a small chapel for himself'; Degas called the movement's goal 'a salon of Realists' (1874 letter to Tissot); in 1886 he dismissed Seurat's Grande Jatte in the one sentence quoted by Pissarro, which Pissarro takes as blindness (the quip is the evidence, not a judgement of Seurat). Moore records him dismissing Bastien-Lepage as 'a Bouguereau of the modern movement' when others were admiring the picture. dual_edged because Caillebotte, Gauguin and Pissarro in the same chronology describe the stance as divisive or unfair. Score reflects several documented episodes from adversarial and friendly sources; not extreme because the Moore and Vollard quips are single-source retellings. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 82/0.7; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (the Boggs catalogue chronology), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
      independent_thinking: [80, 0.66, "d", "D"],
      // [src_degas_vollard1927, src_degas_lemoisne1912, src_degas_huysmans1889, src_degas_boggs1988 | 1873-1912 | dealer memoir + scholar preface + critic + museum catalogue] He controlled the terms on which his work left the studio and was seen. Vollard: he did not like to sell, chose his own frames and refunded sales rather than let a collector change them; he refused permission for a collector to photograph a Delacroix lithograph in his collection ('I don't give a damn about the public'); he refused to be filmed by Sacha Guitry (who had to film him from the street); he would not move into a house with central heating. Lemoisne: his withdrawal from publicity was 'voulue' and he shut out all but a few friends. Huysmans (1889): in a time when painters 'wallow in the trough of crowds' he had exiled himself from exhibitions and public places and shown the 1886 pastels as a parting gesture. Boggs: a critic in April 1880 wrote that he is 'an artist who produces slowly, as he pleases ... without concerning himself about exhibitions and catalogues'; in March 1880 he tried to have the artists' names left off the group-exhibition poster ('When will we stop playing at being stars?'); in April 1873 he dropped plans to show at the Salon. Corroborated by an acquaintance, a scholar, a critic and a museum catalogue. dual_edged because financial pressure drove him to besiege dealers while still guarding control. LEAD REVIEW CORRECTION (downward only, before eligibility was read): was 82/0.7; the corroborating perspectives mostly reach us through one compiling secondary source (the Boggs catalogue chronology), so score and confidence are lowered per the rubric's no-high-score-without-independent-corroboration rule.
