      // [src_degas_lemoisne1912, src_degas_lafond1918, src_degas_vollard1927, src_degas_boggs1988 | 1854-1903 | scholar preface + friendly monograph + dealer memoir + museum catalogue] A long, self-directed apprenticeship and continuing study. Lemoisne: he left law for drawing by 1854, entered the Ecole des Beaux-Arts in April 1855, was in Rome by 1856, and made copies after Poussin, Holbein, Clouet and Lawrence; he knew the Louvre better than any contemporary artist. Lafond: copies after the Italian primitives and Poussin (the Rape of the Sabines on a large scale), Degas following Ingres's advice to copy the masters for 'the sap of the plant'. Vollard has him tell a visiting mother that a young painter should 'copy the masters and re-copy them', and quotes his answer to a critic, 'I have spent my whole life trying to find out [what art is about]'. Boggs: in 1903, at sixty-nine, he wrote 'I must finish this sculpture ... I shall work until I drop' and 'Old age would be sad without work'. Several documented episodes across four sources. It is learning-for-skill, not output counts.
