    rows: {
      // [src_gehrig_sabr_bio, src_gehrig_sabr_krieger_ruth, src_gehrig_sabr_ardolino, src_gehrig_hof | 1927-1937 | secondary biography + footnoted SABR essays + Hall of Fame page quoting a teammate] The SABR biography says that when Joe McCarthy arrived in 1931 and demanded year-round conditioning, this 'mirrored Gehrig's approach': he 'stayed in shape year round and showed up to play every day', and Krieger adds that Gehrig 'appreciated' McCarthy's high expectations. Krieger also reports that in spring 1927 he went to training camp early amid rumours of a right-handed first baseman, while Ruth reported late. Ardolino quotes his 1939 remark that he had averaged ten hours' sleep on almost every night in seventeen years (a self-report, taken as such; Krieger notes the clean-living image was only roughly true, since he smoked and drank in moderation). The Hall of Fame page quotes Bill Dickey: 'He just went out and did his job every day.' Routine, conditioning and punctuality are the evidence; the games-played streak is used only under persistence, and his 1938-39 decline is deliberately not used. Several independent descriptions of the same pattern over a decade; not scored higher because the best-dated instances (1927 camp, 1931 McCarthy) are single retellings and the sleep figure is his own.
      discipline: [74, 0.62, "s", "A"],
      // [src_gehrig_sabr_bio, src_gehrig_sabr_ardolino, src_gehrig_sabr_krieger_ruth | 1921-1937 | secondary biography + footnoted SABR essays] Dated instances before any illness: after the humiliating June 1921 Giants tryout (McGraw: 'Get this fellow out of here!') he went home 'temporarily crushed, but not defeated' and accepted a Hartford contract (SABR biography). In 1930 he played the last three weeks of the season with a broken finger (surgery afterwards, and bone chips found in his elbow) and still appeared in every game. Ardolino, citing Ray Robinson, says that over the 1925-1939 run he broke every finger and had 17 assorted hand fractures that healed unaided (a secondary tally, not independently checked). Ruth's January 1937 attack on the streak ('the worst mistakes a ball player can make') and Gehrig's reply that he had 'never been tired on the field' (Krieger, Ardolino) show he kept playing under open criticism; The SABR biography adds that when writer Dan Daniel told him in 1933 how long the run was, he 'kept right on playing', and that he called it 'a real stunt'. The decline from 1938 and the 1939 ending are excluded: nothing here is inferred from his illness. One dated instance plus corroborating summaries, hence strong_inference.
      persistence: [70, 0.58, "s", "A"],
      // [src_gehrig_sabr_bio, src_gehrig_sabr_krieger_ruth, src_gehrig_sabr_ardolino, src_gehrig_hof, src_gehrig_sabr_july4 | 1925-1935 | secondary biography + SABR essays quoting teammates and writers + Hall of Fame page] The Hall of Fame and the SABR essays describe him as reserved and quiet beside the outgoing Ruth; SABR biography: he 'didn't give writers good quotes'; Lefty Gomez (teammate from 1930): 'how could you ever know how Lou really felt?'; Gehrig himself, 'I'm not a headline guy'. On pay, the SABR biography says he had signed the Yankees' standard contract 'automatically' for years, then let one sit and settled in 1927 for a three-year $25,000 deal, below the figure Ruth had urged, and accepted $23,000 for 1932 against Ruth's reported $80,000; Krieger says it was after his 1933 marriage that Eleanor encouraged him to engage with fans and advertisers and demand what he was worth. Against this, in private he told Ruth to 'control his mouth' and complained about him to reporter Stanley Frank (Krieger), so the reticence was not total; and the feud with Ruth is contested (Gomez says the press inflated it). Style and negotiating conduct, not shyness as a trait read from a quotation. Dual_edged: restraint won him respect but, per the SABR biography, cost him pay and headlines.
      social_assertiveness: [36, 0.55, "s", "D"],
    },
